hey everyone I'm Lucy and joining me  today is Marcus Leto president and game  director over at v1 interactive on  desintegration Marcus hiding I'm doing  well thanks Lucy it's nice to be here so  people know you obviously from your work  on the Halo franchise a few years ago  when you're working a Bungie but then  you kind of left the triple a life  behind went into a more independent game  development lifestyle where and when  were the seeds of disintegrations so  when did you know that you wanted to  move away from halo from that franchise  and make something else and it wasn't  actually too long after I left Bungie I  kind of did some exploration here and  there I looked at big triple-a games and  little mobile games and a lot of  different types of game universes for a  while I spent about two years before I  finally landed on this one idea that had  I had kind of had some sketches for as  far as the fiction is concerned I kept  coming back to it as something that  really interested me and the more I come  back to it the more and intrigued I was  with the universe and the capabilities  that it had and so I decided just to  actually start putting some effort into  building a prototype around that idea  I'm a big sci-fi fan and I think for me  the thing that draws you in to a  particular piece of sci-fi is the world  that's been created so it's really cool  to know that like that was the seed for  it wasn't the gameplay it was creating  this universe can you talk a little bit  more about what the state of the  universe would be as you load into this  integration so the fiction of the story  is set about a hundred fifty years from  now it actually when I while I started  the fiction for this over six years ago  one of the issues that I was dealing  with in this fiction was that humanity  was being pitted against several  different issues that were creating some  severe difficulties and potential  extinction event as well so that was  something that as a result of these  these issues that humanity was dealing  with that taking the actual brain from a  human and integrating that into a  robotic entity was a means of survival  it was it was really meant to be a  temporary solution until real solutions  could be solved and that brain could be  reintegrated with a clone version of  their body and they could become human  again that's the that was that the the  big rosy picture that they painted for  themselves but that didn't work the way  they had planned and so in this future  those that have been integrated some of  them decide to split away and become  this militarized force and they don't  want to become human again  but you and a group of other folks like  you decided to fight back and and  separate from that enjoying with the  natural humans and be part of that you  know that part of humanity is so  important to them and that's who we play  in this game that's where our section  our fiction is set in the future and  that's where we really dive into the  individual characters within the world  and the interest that they have with one  another so I was playing a little bit  last night I was playing through some of  the single-player and what I really  liked about it is that between the  missions and before we get on to the  gameplay itself there are these moments  of respite where you're sort of walking  around I guess your home base and you're  chatting to everyone and just getting  these snippets of the law where did that  idea it reminded me actually of Mass  Effect a little bit you do a mission  then you check on everyone because you'd  want to you want to build up that  rapport with your your crew where did  that come from and how did you start to  build out this crew this motley crew  that you're hanging around with yeah to  that event like we wanted the between  missions for the player to be able to  just come back to a home base a place  where they could you know feel comfort  take a breather get to know some of the  other characters uh it's a it's an  optional kind of thing for the player to  do where they can interact with some of  the other story characters in that  environment and get  little bit more in information from them  about what their motivations are and  such when it comes to those characters  each one of them are like I was saying  there they're unique individuals then  they're not genetically modified super  trained super soldiers or anything like  that they're just normal folks like  journalists teachers mechanics game  developers maybe who knows you know  there's others normal people who are  pitted under extraordinary circumstances  with a will to survive and it's these  characters that while they come from all  walks of life they have this common you  know bond pulling them together this  will to survive and to one day become  human again and so at the beginning of  the game we escaped from the enemy the  Ray on enemy in our game and we are kind  of just pulled together with this group  that they have to get to know each other  and how to work with one another and how  to trust one another throughout the game  and that's where the crux of all the  interesting stuff happens between them  so you've kind of flushed out this world  and you're flushing out these characters  let's talk a little bit about the  gameplay because it is this hybrid of a  first-person shooter if you want it to  be like you're controlling the graph  cycle or it's an RTS essentially you're  commanding units where did that idea  come from  the game actually started out as  strictly an RTS type game it was very  standards banned select micromanage  units on the ground all set within this  universe but it wasn't too long into  that development of a prototype very  very early prototype that I said to  myself this is just never gonna stand  out amongst the sea of other games that  are like this and while I liked what we  were creating I like the simulation of  combat on the ground that was one of our  primary focuses of making that really  interesting to watch  I decided that we needed to do something  dramatically different so it wasn't it  was one of those things late at night  you know when you wake up in the middle  of the night with a with an idea and  this one was turning that camera in the  sky into an active participant in combat  like giving that the player the agency  to involve themselves fully in what's  happening in gameplay so we turned that  camera into a vehicle we now call a grav  cycle and you're the pilot of that graph  cycle with onboard offensive and  defensive weapons but you're also then  commanding those ground units and it  required us to completely rethink how  you can interact with those ground units  in a way that doesn't overwhelm the  player and is in keeping with the  fluidity of that first person shooter  mechanic and so that took us quite a  long time to iterate on that gameplay  cycle and really hone in and find that  core nugget that was just golden that  turned into something really cool and  special that took us a long time to  finally get to a place where we were  excited and comfortable you know with  the with the Corps combat loop and those  core gameplay mechanics but because  there's nothing else out there like this  so we couldn't go chase the tail of  another game we had to invent all new  Mechanics for how that works and and I  think we got it down to this this really  nice fluid of gameplay experience that  while it's a little hard to describe I  think once players jump into the game  and get a get a hands-on experience with  it I think they'll really appreciate  what what we've created it's a strange  thing to have to pitch to people because  you're you can pitch to the RTS crowd  and you can pitch to the first person to  to crowd but you can also encourage them  that you can you could swap sides you  can do either one what what was the  moment can you describe the moment where  things clicked in terms of the gameplay  it it took some time when we were going  through this iteration cycle of trying  to figure out how  to command those ground units initially  we were still trying to allow  micromanagement of them and it wasn't  until we said let's just let go of that  and let's boil things down to their bare  essence the most important parts of what  we really want to get across and then we  we got to this point where we turned  that ground crew into a more into a  solid entity themselves like it might be  as few as two ground units and as as  many as four each of whom have a unique  ability that you can use tactically in  gameplay but we wanted to make sure that  they did smart contextual things on the  ground based on your decisions that you  give them your commands that you give  them and we do that through a command  pulse and it was the invention of this  command pulse that literally fires out  of your graph cycled down onto the  ground you can position them in the  environment using that you can target a  priority target and enemy you can even  select objectives within in the  environment such as things that they  need to interact with for story purposes  or for picking up stuff like Salvage in  the game that you use as a currency when  you come back to that home base and so  it wasn't until we got to that point  where we started really honing in on how  that mechanic works and how smart those  AI needed to be on the ground then  finally it all began to click and we  knew that I think we were getting to the  homestretch then of what we needed to do  on the design side so you said you had  this idea six years ago what was the  process like of building the team to  execute the idea it took a while because  I didn't want to go to a publisher and  do a whole bunch of hand waving and  smoke and mirrors or just a PowerPoint  presentation saying hey it's just me and  I'd like to build a studio and this game  idea so I hired a couple college  students who were still in their senior  year  and to help me out on the engineering  side and we took our time to slowly go  through this process of building a  prototype that we could use as a solid  example for the design mechanics and for  the overall intent of what we wanted to  do with the game that made it a lot  easier for us to start pitching it  around to publishers like Microsoft and  Sony and such but it wasn't until I met  one of my old colleagues from Bungie his  name's Alan and Alan and I met for lunch  and he was starting to work with this  new publisher out of New York part of  take two they hadn't named themselves  yet and they were like he's like I think  you should pitch the game to to to our  group because what you're doing what you  want to do is build in moderate size  tripl a quality game with a small team  that has the highest quality possible--  and a modest budget and that's exactly  what they were looking for so it was  kind of like this perfect union of my  and and perfect timing you know for for  us to meet up with him and for us to  then get involved with private division  which was ultimately the what the studio  or the publisher called themselves and  and it took a while you know we had to  go through a greenlight process even  with them of proving that we could grow  our team I could hire on a handful of  other people that I know from the  industry as well as hire a brand new  talent straight out of school and so  while we were only like 10 to 15 people  for a majority of the development time  it's not until just this last year that  we've grown to about 30 people now so  last question I mean if you were to go  back and see yourself six years ago and  say you know we're on the cusp of  disintegration coming out now what would  you tell yourself and what are your  hopes for the game for the studio I  wouldn't have believed that we would  have gotten this far already  it seems like it's been a long time but  I initially was planning on the studio  being super small and us being like this  teeny little indie boutique thing I am  so happy and so proud for how the studio  has evolved over the course of the last  three and a half years to where we have  grown with such great talent and we have  built such an amazing game in my opinion  of course I'm really proud of what we've  created together we took a leap of faith  to do something completely different and  new and it was a challenge for sure it  was not easy and it still isn't easy as  we try to wrap up the game amidst a  global pandemic and everybody is working  from home so it's a really interesting  time for us this is certainly one of  those most memorable dev cycles I've  ever been in that is one for the history  books for sure but we made it work and  I'm really really happy and proud of v1  the studio and uh and what we've been  able to create as a result thank you so  much Marcus for more on disintegration  make sure you stay tuned to game spot  and don't forget we're running play for  all all summer for about the next six  weeks or so talking about all the newest  game announcements as well as doing a  charity drive for black lives matter and  Direct Relief there will be donation  links below  you  [Music]

